From eed4580b9bb1b56d7719eab52b67f471a9c9765a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gpu-mesa <gpu-mesa@t1100.local>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:15:35 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: t1100: enable the Mali-G72 (panfrost), without
 GPU DVFS

The GPU node comes from mt8183.dtsi and was only switched off in the board
DTS: gpu@13040000 with "mediatek,mt8183b-mali", the mfgcfg clock gate
(CLK_MFG_BG3D at 0x13000000), the three MFG power domains ("core0",
"core1", "core2") and the mt8183 GPU OPP table.  Nothing else is needed for
panfrost to bind - it has its own MMU (no iommus/iommu node), resets are
optional (the node has none, as on mt8183) and the "speed-bin" nvmem cell
is optional too (panfrost_read_speedbin(): -ENOENT means "no speedbins").

Cross-checks for MT6771:
 - GPU 0x13040000, SPI 278 (job), 279 (mmu), 280 (gpu) and mfgcfg
   0x13000000 match the stock device tree
   (refs/stock-mt6771.dts, "mali@13040000" / "mfgcfg@13000000") and
   docs/research.md;
 - the vendor gpufreq node lists exactly the five MTCMOS of mt8183's MFG
   hierarchy - mtcmos_mfg_async, mtcmos_mfg, mtcmos_mfg_core0,
   mtcmos_mfg_core1, mtcmos_mfg_core2 - which is the strongest available
   evidence that the mt8183 scpsys domain IDs and offsets apply here
   (there is no public MT6771 TRM);
 - the MFG_2D bus protection writes the same infracfg registers
   (TOP_AXI_PROT_EN_1_*/EN_*) that the already working DISP domain uses;
 - refs/affenull-mt6771 (upstream MT6771 port, PennyOS on Planet Cosmo) and
   refs/mt6771-next (Arseniy Velikanov's port of this very tablet) both
   leave &gpu enabled with nothing but mali-supply.

DVFS: operating-points-v2 is dropped on purpose.  The vendor driver keeps
VSRAM_GPU exactly 100 mV above VGPU and changes both together (stock 4.14
log, refs/device/dmesg-stock.txt.gz: "VGPU is enabled = 1 (900 mV),
VSRAM_GPU is enabled = 1 (1000 mV)" and "current freq = 800000 KHz,
current volt = 825000 uV ... g_cur_opp_vsram_volt = 92500").  panfrost's
mt8183b variant knows a single supply and the generic regulator coupler
cannot be used on this PMIC (it needs always-on rails and mt6358's probe
aborts on -ENOTSUPP), so letting the OPP table drive VGPU down to 625 mV
would leave VSRAM at 1000 mV - a 375 mV split where the hardware allows
100 mV.  Without an OPP table panfrost skips devfreq
(panfrost_devfreq_init() returns after -ENODEV) and only enables VGPU at
the voltage the bootloader left; the GPU runs at the rate the mfg mux
already has (512 MHz in the stock log, before gpufreq raised it to 800),
which is enough for GBM/EGL bring-up.  sram-supply goes away with it: the
mt8183b variant never requests it (the binding only allows it for the
non-b "mediatek,mt8183-mali" compatible,
Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpu/arm,mali-bifrost.yaml), and VSRAM_GPU
is kept alive by regulator-always-on instead.

Not verified on hardware yet: this image has not been flashed.

Signed-off-by: gpu-mesa <gpu-mesa@t1100.local>
---
 .../boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts  | 63 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 46 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts
index bdca86e76..0399b3016 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts
@@ -603,29 +603,51 @@ &u2port0 {
 };
 
 /*
- * GPU: Mali-G72 MP3, identical core to MT8183, so panfrost binds through
- * "mediatek,mt8183b-mali". VGPU is the buck that feeds the shader cores,
- * VSRAM_GPU its SRAM rail; panfrost enables sram first and raises VGPU
- * afterwards (panfrost_regulator_init()), which is why the two rails are
- * described as separate supplies instead of being tied together with
- * regulator-coupled-with. The generic coupler only accepts *always-on*
- * regulators (drivers/regulator/core.c: "Coupling of a non always-on
- * regulator is unimplemented"), and mt6358_regulator_probe() aborts on the
- * first rail it cannot register - one -ENOTSUPP for VGPU therefore removed
- * *every* MT6358 rail, which is what kept eMMC, USB and the whole MM stack
- * in deferred probe. Same wiring as refs/affenull-mt6771 (MT6771 upstream
- * port) and mt8183-kukui.dtsi.
+ * GPU: Mali-G72 MP3, the same Bifrost core as MT8183, so panfrost binds
+ * through "mediatek,mt8183b-mali" inherited from mt8183.dtsi. That variant
+ * consumes a single supply, "mali" (VGPU), and expects the three MFG power
+ * domains ("core0", "core1", "core2", with MFG_ASYNC and MFG as their
+ * parents). VSRAM_GPU is the SRAM rail of the same block: it is *not*
+ * requested by the driver, so it is kept on with regulator-always-on below -
+ * drop that and regulator_late_cleanup() kills the GPU SRAM.
  *
- * Still off: enabling the GPU powers up MFG, which is not needed for
- * eMMC/USB/KDE bring-up and is the only consumer that writes the MFG
- * registers nobody has verified on MT6771 yet.
+ * Regulator coupling is deliberately not used: the generic coupler only
+ * accepts *always-on* regulators (drivers/regulator/core.c, "Coupling of a
+ * non always-on regulator is unimplemented") and mt6358_regulator_probe()
+ * aborts on the first rail it cannot register, so one -ENOTSUPP for VGPU
+ * removed *every* MT6358 rail and kept eMMC, USB and the whole MM stack in
+ * deferred probe (docs/bringup-log.md, 30.10).
+ *
+ * DVFS is off for the first bring-up: without operating-points-v2 panfrost
+ * skips devfreq/OPP entirely (panfrost_devfreq_init() -> -ENODEV -> "continue
+ * without devfreq") and only enables VGPU through panfrost_regulator_init(),
+ * leaving both rails at the boot values the bootloader and the vendor
+ * gpufreq driver leave behind. The stock 4.14 log (refs/device/dmesg-stock)
+ * prints those values and the reason they must not be moved independently:
+ *
+ *   VGPU is enabled = 1 (900 mV), VSRAM_GPU is enabled = 1 (1000 mV)
+ *   current freq = 800000 KHz, current volt = 825000 uV
+ *        g_cur_opp_volt = 82500, g_cur_opp_vsram_volt = 92500
+ *
+ * i.e. the vendor keeps VSRAM exactly 100 mV above VGPU (the limit
+ * mt8183-kukui encodes as regulator-coupled-max-spread = <100000>) and moves
+ * both rails together. We cannot: one supply per driver + no coupler, so an
+ * OPP-driven VGPU drop to 625-825 mV would leave VSRAM at 1000 mV, i.e. up to
+ * 375 mV apart. At probe the mfg mux reads 512 MHz (the vendor driver read
+ * 512001 kHz before it raised the rate to 800 MHz), which is a perfectly
+ * usable GPU clock, so nothing is lost by not touching it: the GPU runs at
+ * the rate the bootloader left. Revisit DVFS once VSRAM can be driven
+ * together with VGPU (always-on coupling or a OPP table with both rails).
  */
 &gpu {
 	mali-supply = <&mt6358_vgpu_reg>;
-	sram-supply = <&mt6358_vsram_gpu_reg>;
-	status = "disabled";
+	/delete-property/ operating-points-v2;
+	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/* Only the GPU used it; leaving it behind would invite a DVFS enable by accident. */
+/delete-node/ &gpu_opp_table;
+
 /*
  * The two MFG power domains carry MTK_SCPD_DOMAIN_SUPPLY, and since 6.12
  * mtk-pm-domains looks the regulator up in the *domain* node itself
@@ -643,6 +665,13 @@ &mfg {
 	domain-supply = <&mt6358_vgpu_reg>;
 };
 
+/*
+ * Bounds from the vendor tables: the GPU OPPs top out at 800 MHz / 825 mV
+ * VGPU, the bootloader hands the kernel 900 mV VGPU and 1000 mV VSRAM_GPU
+ * (both printed by the stock gpufreq probe, see &gpu above). Nothing in the
+ * mainline tree writes these rails today - panfrost only enables VGPU - but
+ * the limits keep a future DVFS table from going outside the PMIC's range.
+ */
 &mt6358_vgpu_reg {
 	regulator-min-microvolt = <625000>;
 	regulator-max-microvolt = <900000>;
-- 
2.53.0

